1
00:00:01.926 --> 00:00:07.840
It is now 2026, and prompting AI models is very different from when CAT GPT first came out in 2022.

2
00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:14.880
Using AI well is one of the most impactful skills you can develop. And people that are not yet at

3
00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:21.440
the cutting edge of AI usage often run into AI generating frustrating outputs. I want to make

4
00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:27.360
sure you're an expert prompter and can take advantage of today's AI tools, which are much

5
00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:33.759
more powerful than they were even a year ago. Let's take a look at two different experiences,

6
00:00:33.759 --> 00:00:41.919
the AI novice and the AI power user. Many AI experts have learned to use it to answer hard

7
00:00:41.919 --> 00:00:48.639
questions. In contrast, many people, including AI novices, may have gotten used to using AI for

8
00:00:48.639 --> 00:00:54.080
simple questions, as if you were prompting it like a Google search. So you ask it,

9
00:00:54.080 --> 00:01:00.240
does Taco Bell still have the double decker taco? And maybe you get an answer like that,

10
00:01:01.279 --> 00:01:09.199
which is fine. But if you have much harder questions, you can also ask it of the AI and

11
00:01:09.199 --> 00:01:15.760
give it time to think. For example, if you're looking to buy a car, you can upload to most

12
00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:22.800
of the commercial services like CAT GPT, Gemini, Anthropics Cloud, or others, a set of documents,

13
00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:28.000
including car specs, quotes, insurance plans, and ask it, what are the trade-offs for these

14
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:34.000
different cars I'm thinking about? And tell it to read everything and to think hard before answering.

15
00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:41.040
And this can cause AI to spend many seconds or even minutes to think and then compile a

16
00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:45.919
detailed report for you. I find this a huge time saver for a lot of things I have to do.

17
00:01:46.879 --> 00:01:53.199
Another example, AI power users have learned to provide the right context or the right background

18
00:01:53.199 --> 00:02:00.400
information to the AI to set it up for successfully answering your question. In contrast, I see some

19
00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:07.839
AI novices use a short prompt and hope that AI will fill in the blanks. But if you think of AI

20
00:02:07.839 --> 00:02:14.320
as maybe being akin to a really smart, fresh college grad, highly motivated, but that doesn't

21
00:02:14.639 --> 00:02:19.600
really know that much about you yet, then a short prompt sometimes doesn't give it enough

22
00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:25.919
information or enough background context to answer your question accurately. So if you tell AI,

23
00:02:25.919 --> 00:02:32.000
please write a good self-review to send to my boss. The AI doesn't know what you've actually

24
00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:36.479
done over the last year because you haven't told it yet. And it might write a very generic

25
00:02:36.479 --> 00:02:43.199
self-review, which isn't that helpful. In contrast, I find that AI power users

26
00:02:43.199 --> 00:02:48.559
almost have empathy for the AI. I don't want to overly anthropomorphize the AI,

27
00:02:48.559 --> 00:02:53.839
but if you could put yourself in the shoes of someone getting a set of instructions from you,

28
00:02:53.839 --> 00:02:58.639
you can ask yourself, will they actually know enough about you to do a good job on the task

29
00:02:58.639 --> 00:03:04.399
you're assigning them? So an AI power user in comparison might upload a lot of information

30
00:03:04.399 --> 00:03:09.119
to the AI, maybe give it a screenshot of a project tracker, showing what you've worked on,

31
00:03:09.679 --> 00:03:15.759
recent project dogs, maybe voice memo notes where you talk through the projects and then tell it to

32
00:03:15.759 --> 00:03:22.559
write a self-review to send to my boss. And that could do a much better job capturing what you're

33
00:03:22.559 --> 00:03:30.880
most proud of. One of the things power users have learned to do is how to prompt AI to get honest

34
00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:38.080
feedback. A big problem with AI is it often wants to please you. In fact, many AI systems were

35
00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:45.679
trained to try to make the users happy. And if you ask it a biased question, it will often give

36
00:03:45.679 --> 00:03:52.399
a biased answer because it's trying to tell you what it thinks you want to hear. For example,

37
00:03:52.399 --> 00:03:59.759
if you say, I have a great business idea, mobile tie dyeing, critique it. Because you called it

38
00:03:59.759 --> 00:04:06.000
a great business idea and you're saying it's your idea, the AI will naturally want to please you and

39
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:14.160
say, what a great idea. We sometimes call this sycophancy. And it's well known that if you give

40
00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:19.600
even a hint of what answer you're hoping for, there's a good chance the AI will just reflect

41
00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:27.200
back your preferences or your preconceptions. In contrast, AI power users tend to ask neutral

42
00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:32.239
questions that don't give any hint to the AI for what answer you're hoping for or not hoping for.

43
00:04:32.959 --> 00:04:40.000
Or if you give it a rubric or grading criteria to tell the AI how to form the basis for its answer,

44
00:04:40.559 --> 00:04:46.880
that also forces it to be more objective. For example, if you were to say, please analyze the

45
00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:53.040
following business idea objectively, mobile tie dyeing. And don't just make up a bunch of things

46
00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:59.119
for what you think. Use the rubric of the grading criteria above, such as, is there a problem? Is

47
00:04:59.119 --> 00:05:04.079
there a market? Do I have a competitive advantage? If you give instructions like this to the AI,

48
00:05:04.079 --> 00:05:09.040
then the AI doesn't know. Are you hoping it'll tell you it's a great idea or that it will save

49
00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:14.320
you from spending a lot of time on the bad business idea? And it's much more likely to then

50
00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:20.640
tell you something like, oh, this idea is an 8 out of 100 and also why the score's low.

51
00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:28.160
In case you run a mobile tie dye business, I wish you really best of luck. And AI could also

52
00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:33.440
ask some useful questions to help you think through how to make the business even better.

53
00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:41.040
Lastly, I found that AI novices and AI users ask AI to write in very different ways.

54
00:05:41.679 --> 00:05:47.040
Novices will just ask AI to write stuff, like write a blog post about the BlackBerry.

55
00:05:48.079 --> 00:05:53.440
And it will generate a bunch of text that maybe looks like this, which sounds like AI slop.

56
00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:58.880
It's a bunch of generic text that's just not that interesting and takes up a lot of space.

57
00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:06.880
In contrast, an AI power user will often not ask the AI system to just jump in writing directly,

58
00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:13.600
but instead ask AI to first outline an article and then critique the outline and maybe iterate

59
00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:18.559
a few times with the outline to shape the article. And only then ask AI to start to

60
00:06:18.559 --> 00:06:24.959
draft the final article. So give it a set of uploaded notes as context. An expert may say,

61
00:06:24.959 --> 00:06:29.760
outline a blog post about the BlackBerry based on my notes so it knows what you want to talk about.

62
00:06:30.799 --> 00:06:37.440
And the AI may start by giving an outline. And you might then give feedback to the AI about what

63
00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:42.640
you like and what you don't like about the outline. And even iterate a few times, have a

64
00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:47.679
few back and forth rounds before you have an outline that you're satisfied with. And maybe

65
00:06:47.679 --> 00:06:52.799
only then expand the outline into bullet points. And maybe even go back and forth a few times to

66
00:06:52.799 --> 00:06:59.519
critique the bullet points before you're satisfied with that, and then expand it into the final text.

67
00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:06.559
This type of power user workflow is much more likely to generate some text that you're happy

68
00:07:06.559 --> 00:07:13.920
with, as opposed to AI slop. And in this type of workflow, you're treating the AI as a thinking

69
00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:19.920
partner to almost help you brainstorm and explore different options for what you might want to write.

70
00:07:20.799 --> 00:07:28.079
AI systems do make mistakes, but maybe fewer than most people think, especially if you prompt it

71
00:07:28.079 --> 00:07:36.480
well. They made a lot more mistakes back in 2022 or 2023 than they do now. But a lot of widely

72
00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:42.399
publicized mistakes that AI has made, some of which went viral on social media, has made people

73
00:07:42.399 --> 00:07:49.040
think that AI maybe makes even more mistakes than it actually does. There's a well-puzzle-sized one

74
00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:54.000
where people asked it how many R's are there in the word strawberry, and it thinks there are two R's.

75
00:07:54.559 --> 00:08:00.559
And here's one that I found amusing. I want to wash my car. Should I walk or drive there? And

76
00:08:00.559 --> 00:08:07.040
the AI says, walk, which would leave you there while you wash your car. But these viral examples are not

77
00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:14.799
representative of AI capabilities. In contrast, PAR users know that AI can deliver significant

78
00:08:14.799 --> 00:08:21.679
value through tasks like doing deep research and writing research reports, or taking your personal

79
00:08:21.679 --> 00:08:29.119
data, like your health or heart rate or running time data, and analyzing that for you. Or something

80
00:08:29.119 --> 00:08:36.479
we'll talk about later, even building websites for you. I've seen being an AI PAR user

81
00:08:36.479 --> 00:08:42.799
tremendously benefit individuals, as well as their businesses. It'll save you time and improve

82
00:08:42.799 --> 00:08:49.760
your professional and personal lives. It'll help you to build lots of cool things. You'll learn how later

83
00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:57.599
in these videos. And being able to prompt AI at an expert level is a highly in-demand job skill,

84
00:08:57.599 --> 00:09:04.239
no matter what job role you're in. In the rest of these videos, I hope to take you from wherever

85
00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:13.359
you are today to being an AI PAR user. Much is said about AI being useful. I find using AI

86
00:09:13.359 --> 00:09:20.559
really fun as well, and you'll see a few examples of that in these videos too. Now, one foundational

87
00:09:20.559 --> 00:09:26.479
piece of knowledge that helps you work with AI is understanding where it gets its knowledge from,

88
00:09:27.119 --> 00:09:31.840
so that you can better predict when it'll get something right, and when you maybe shouldn't

89
00:09:31.840 --> 00:09:39.200
count on its answer. Let's go on to the next video to learn about how AI gets its knowledge.
